---
/**
 * foodcraft / GalleryBO — "Sipsip Tea Bar" (BO), the counter in photographs.
 *
 * ROLE: the one section on the page that is nothing but the owner's own
 * pictures. A CENTRED head — the lilac pill eyebrow over one Baloo 2 headline
 * carrying this template's purple colour-emphasis — sitting above the design's
 * SCATTERED four-up: a row of soft photo tiles where every other tile is
 * dropped 26px and four of the eight corners are blown out to 60px, so the grid
 * reads as hand-placed rather than ruled. Nothing in it has a sharp corner.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every number below is lifted verbatim from
 * the element carrying data-component="GALLERY" in
 * "Sipsip Tea Bar Landing.dc.html":
 *   head        text-align center · margin-bottom 30px
 *   eyebrow     inline-block PILL · 11px / 700 / .14em / uppercase ·
 *               #5C3580 on a #EEE2F5 ground · padding 8px 16px · radius 100px
 *                                                        → the wrapper's .kick
 *   headline    Baloo 2 700 clamp(30px,4vw,52px)/1.08 · margin `.24em 0 0` ·
 *               #241A22, with a #7B4FA8 <span> emphasis   → the wrapper's .h2
 *   grid        repeat(4, minmax(0,1fr)) · gap 14px
 *   tile        height 250px · overflow hidden · ground #F0E4F6  → .plate
 *   stagger     margin-top 26px on tiles 2, 4, 6, 8 (0 on 1, 3, 5, 7)
 *   corners     26px on every tile, ONE corner opened to 60px on four of them:
 *               tile 2 bottom-right · 3 top-left · 5 top-right · 8 bottom-left
 *   caption     absolute 10px/10px · #FFF7F2 ground · #241A22 label · 11px /
 *               700 · padding 6px 13px · radius 100px          → .plate .cap
 * The stagger and the blown corners are the whole character of this section and
 * are reproduced exactly; neither has been flattened to suit a list of unknown
 * length (see THE COUNT LADDER).
 *
 * CLASS NAMES ARE THIS TEMPLATE'S OWN. An audit had to rename a whole set of
 * classes copied verbatim from a sibling, so nothing here reuses GalleryBM's
 * `.tcg-*` or GalleryBN's `.sec-*`: every scoped class is prefixed `.sip-gal-`,
 * matching AboutBO's `.sip-about-` for this same template. Only the HOUSE STYLE
 * crossed over — the doc block, the tolerant reads, the guard discipline, the
 * count ladder as an idea. No palette, no layout and no class name did.
 *
 * R1 · NO LITERAL HEX. Every hex the design writes here is used through the
 * PageBO token holding the same value:
 *   #EEE2F5 --lilac · #5C3580 --accent-deep · #241A22 --ink ·
 *   #FFF7F2 --paper · #7B4FA8 --accent (the emphasis, via the wrapper) ·
 *   26px --r-lg · 100px --r-pill
 *   ONE COLOUR HAS NO EXACT TOKEN and is reported rather than invented: the
 *   design's tile ground is #F0E4F6, and no PageBO token holds that value. The
 *   nearest is --well / --lilac (#EEE2F5, each channel within 2/255), which is
 *   what .plate already paints, so the plate takes the token and no hex is
 *   written. Identical to the call AboutBO made for the same ground. Flagged so
 *   nobody reads it as a miss.
 *   THE 60px CORNER AND THE 250px HEIGHT are LENGTHS, not colours. PageBO's
 *   radius scale is 100 / 26 / 18 and holds no 60, so the blown corner is
 *   stated as a local custom property (--sip-gal-corner) rather than pretending
 *   a token exists. R1 governs colour, and no colour LITERAL appears here: the
 *   four colours this file sets — --accent-deep, --lilac, --paper, --ink — are
 *   every one of them a token reference.
 *   THE PAIRING RISK IN THIS PALETTE DOES NOT ARISE HERE: this section sits on
 *   the plain --paper ground and opts into none of .band-pink / .band-sage /
 *   .band-lilac, so no purple is ever set on the pink. The only purple that
 *   appears is on the blush paper (5.63:1) and on the lilac pill (7.40:1).
 *
 * ── EDITABLE HOOKS (each one DECLARED in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts
 *    under the `gallery` group, whose entire declaration is three fields —
 *    gallery.tag, gallery.headline, and the gallery.items LIST with its two
 *    item fields (image, caption) — which is what the four hook paths below
 *    resolve to, so every data-field is a hook a real sidebar input owns) ────
 *   gallery.tag                        → the lilac pill eyebrow (.kick)
 *   gallery.headline                   → the display h2, set:html
 *   gallery.items.<original i>.image   → data-image-field, one per tile
 *   gallery.items.<original i>.caption → the optional cream caption pill
 * That is the whole list. A data-field on any other path is a dead hook no
 * sidebar input owns, so no other path is written here.
 *
 * ── R2 · NOTHING VISIBLE IS SUPPLIED BY THIS FILE. Not one word. The design's
 * demo copy — "Gallery", "Straight from the counter." and the eight slot
 * placeholders ("Classic", "Taro", "The bar", "Pearls", "Fruit tea", "Matcha",
 * "The corner", "To go") — is the DESIGNER filling their own template. Those
 * eight are captions for photographs nobody has looked at, so they are neither
 * hard-coded nor invented as caption defaults: an uncaptioned photo is simply a
 * photo, and that is also what derive-content-defaults ships (it seeds images
 * and omits the caption key entirely). Every guard sits OUTSIDE its tag, so an
 * empty value removes the ELEMENT: no empty eyebrow pill, no headline over
 * nothing, no head block with nothing in it, and no empty cream pill sitting on
 * a photograph.
 * NO STAR ROW EXISTS IN THIS SECTION. The design's GALLERY carries no rating
 * and the `gallery` group exposes no numeric field, so there is nothing here
 * that could print five stars for someone who gave no number.
 *
 * ── DERIVED (computed here, never invented) ─────────────────────────────────
 *   · TILE INDEXES (R6). Rows with no image are dropped and the ORIGINAL array
 *     index travels with the survivor as `.i`. The editor writes the data-field
 *     path verbatim into the draft, so a re-indexed path would patch a
 *     different row: clearing row 2's photo would misdirect row 3's caption
 *     edits into row 2.
 *   · THE STAGGER AND THE CORNERS ARE DECORATIVE, so they key off the DISPLAY
 *     position (R6), not the original index — the design's rhythm is "every
 *     other tile drops", and it must stay every other tile after a blank row is
 *     removed. The corner pattern cycles at 8, which is the design's own run
 *     length, so a ninth photograph restarts it rather than running out.
 *   · gallery.items is read tolerantly: the object form, the legacy bare ARRAY
 *     at content.gallery itself (both shapes reach production), and a bare
 *     string inside the list treated as an image path. A `.map is not a
 *     function` costs the whole page, not one tile.
 *   · the image url has its apostrophes percent-escaped before it goes into
 *     url('…'), so an upload named "sipsip's counter.jpg" cannot close the
 *     quote early and take the whole declaration down with it. The RAW value
 *     still travels into data-image-field, which is what the editor reads back.
 *   · THE COUNT LADDER. The design hard-codes eight slots; content does not.
 *     derive-content-defaults hands over up to six photos and an owner may
 *     upload one or eleven, so the design's four-up is the TOP of a ladder:
 *       1–3   the grid narrows to exactly that many tracks, so a short gallery
 *             is three full tiles rather than three tiles and a hole
 *       4–8   the design's four tracks, exactly
 *       any   when the last row does not divide by the track count, the FINAL
 *             tile stretches over whatever tracks its row left empty, so the
 *             grid never ends ragged. Six photos — the default build — is the
 *             case this exists for: four across, then one tile and one wide.
 *     The stretch is computed PER BREAKPOINT (a four-track row and a two-track
 *     row run out at different counts) and is reset entirely at one column.
 *
 * ── DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED, so none of these reads as an oversight ───────
 *   · NO onPage() ANCHOR HELPER (R7). This section renders no href of any kind:
 *     the design's GALLERY has no button, no link and no action, and the
 *     `gallery` schema group declares no href field, so PAGE_ANCHORS /
 *     ANCHOR_ALIAS would have nothing to remap and would ship as dead
 *     frontmatter that later drifts out of step with the wrapper. If a link is
 *     ever added here it must arrive WITH the helper copied in from HeaderBO,
 *     and for THIS page that means PAGE_ANCHORS = menu / about / gallery /
 *     visit / faq / main, with ANCHOR_ALIAS mapping location + contact → visit,
 *     services + rooms → menu, host + why → about, work → gallery, book →
 *     main, and off-page hrefs (tel: mailto: http) passing through untouched.
 *     The anchor duty this file DOES own is its id="gallery" — one of PageBO's
 *     six page anchors, the target the header nav links at, and the target the
 *     `work` alias resolves to.
 *   · NO BUTTONS (R4), because the design's GALLERY has none. So none of
 *     .btn--fill / .btn--pink / .btn--cream / .btn--line appears here, nor does
 *     any engine-pinned name (.btn-primary .btn-ghost .nav-cta .cta-band .trust
 *     .testi, or `.hero` on a <section>).
 *   · THE DESIGN'S OWN HEADLINE SIZE (clamp(30px,4vw,52px)/1.08). PageBO's
 *     shared .h2 is clamp(28px,3.9vw,52px)/1.12 — the same 52px cap — and
 *     forking a font-size into one of fifteen sections would leave this
 *     headline out of step with the other fourteen. The wrapper normalised it;
 *     the wrapper keeps it. Only the design's MARGIN is set here.
 *   · SECTION PADDING. The design writes `padding:80px 28px 0`: the 1200/gutter
 *     half is the shared .wrap (min(1200px, 92vw)) and the top is the wrapper's
 *     `section { padding: clamp(48px,6.4vw,80px) 0 }`, which caps at exactly
 *     the design's 80px. The design's bottom-0 is a WHOLE-PAGE rhythm decision
 *     (every unbanded section in it pads top only) and belongs in the wrapper's
 *     shared rule; one section zeroing its own bottom while fourteen siblings
 *     keep theirs is the local re-derivation that puts a template out of step
 *     with its design. Flagged, not done here.
 *
 * ── THREE DECISIONS WORTH SEEING ───────────────────────────────────────────
 *   · EVERY TILE RULE IS QUALIFIED WITH .sip-gal-grid, and the reason has to be
 *     stated correctly, because an earlier draft of this block stated it
 *     backwards. HOW THIS PROJECT SCOPES: Astro's `scopedStyleStrategy` defaults
 *     to 'attribute' and astro-site-template/astro.config.mjs overrides nothing,
 *     so the BUILD emits `.sip-gal-frame[data-astro-cid-…]` — one attribute
 *     selector on every scoped compound, worth (0,1,0) each. dist/index.html
 *     carries data-astro-cid and contains no `:where(.astro-` at all. (Calling
 *     @astrojs/compiler.transform() directly shows `:where(.astro-…)` instead,
 *     because the bare compiler API defaults to 'where'; that output is NOT what
 *     ships, and mistaking it for "the compiler's emitted CSS" is what produced
 *     the earlier claim.)
 *     So against the wrapper's `html[data-page="BO"] :where(.plate)` at (0,1,1)
 *     and `… :where(.plate) .cap` at (0,2,1), the rules below ship at (0,4,0)
 *     and (0,6,0). Nothing here is one class away from losing — a lone
 *     `.sip-gal-frame` would already weigh (0,2,0) and win. The qualifiers stay
 *     because they read consistently and because they would still hold if
 *     scopedStyleStrategy were ever set to 'where'.
 *   · THE CAPTION PILL IS THE DESIGN'S, NOT THE PRIMITIVE'S DEFAULT. PageBO's
 *     .plate .cap is an opaque --ink pill with an --on-ink label; the GALLERY
 *     element draws the inverse — an opaque --paper pill with an --ink label,
 *     at 10px/10px and 6px/13px instead of 12px/12px and 7px/14px. The class is
 *     still .cap, so the position, z-index, family and wrap machinery stay
 *     shared, and only those five design values are restated — plus a max-width
 *     derived from the design's own 10px offsets, so a long caption wraps inside
 *     the tile rather than running past it. BOTH pills are fully opaque, so both
 *     hold their own contrast over any photograph whatever; this one measures
 *     --ink on --paper, computed below. The design is the specification, so the
 *     design's pill is what ships.
 *   · THE HOVER LIFT is the only thing here the static design file could not
 *     express. PageBO ships `transition: transform .5s ease` on .ph, which is
 *     dead weight unless a section moves it, and a photo grid is where it
 *     belongs. It is a 1.03 scale INSIDE an already-clipped plate, so the
 *     frame, its blown corner and its caption never move, and reduced-motion
 *     cancels it.
 *
 * ── R5 · THE SECTION GATE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * The section renders only when at least one row carries an IMAGE — the BODY
 * content, never the tag or the headline. lib/derive-content-defaults.ts sets
 * gallery.tag ('Inside') and gallery.headline ('A look at our work') on EVERY
 * site, so a gate counting those would ship an eyebrow and a display headline
 * over an empty grid. Once photographs exist the tag and headline render (still
 * individually guarded) — refusing them would strand two live sidebar inputs.
 *
 * ── R9 · ACCESSIBILITY ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * A tile is announced only when the owner gave it words. With a caption the
 * photo layer is role="img" named by THAT CAPTION — the owner's own words, and
 * also the visible pill. With no caption the layer is HIDDEN from the tree
 * rather than announced as "image"; inventing "Tea bar photo" would publish a
 * description of something nobody described. The two attributes are mutually
 * exclusive and BOTH conditional, so no literal aria-hidden ever sits beside a
 * conditional one — the duplicated-attribute bug earlier components shipped,
 * where the literal wins and hides content that should have been announced.
 * Nothing else here is decorative: the stagger and the blown corners are CSS,
 * so there is no numeral and no icon that could need hiding.
 *
 * ── R3 · one scoped style block, no is:global. Astro's scoped styles cannot
 * reach elements created by set:html, so the owner's own <em> inside the
 * headline is styled by PageBO's global `:where(.h2 em)` rule — font-style
 * normal and --accent, a COLOUR change, because Baloo 2 has no italic and
 * asking for one synthesises an oblique that fights its rounded terminals — and
 * NOT from here. Anything this file ever needed to say about an owner <em>
 * would have to go through :global().
 *
 * ── R8 · RESPONSIVE. THIS FILE STATES NO CLAMP AND NO FONT-SIZE AT ANY WIDTH,
 * so the inversion two audits found — a floor in the 900 query sitting under a
 * cap in the 520 query — has nothing here to happen to. Every metric it does
 * own was traced across all three widths:
 *   tracks     4 · 2 (≤900) · 1 (≤520)          — the collapse R8 asks for
 *   gap        14px flat
 *   tile       250px flat. Width follows .wrap = min(1200px, 92vw) less the
 *              gaps: ~265×250 at a 1200px viewport (.wrap is 1104px there, not
 *              1200), ~290×250 once .wrap reaches its 1200px cap at ~1304px and
 *              wider, ~407×250 just inside the 900 collapse (828px of wrap, two
 *              tracks, one 14px gap), ~331×250 at 360 — never taller, never
 *              shorter
 *   stagger    26px · 26px · 0 (≤520, where a drop is only a second gap in a
 *              single column) — monotonically down
 *   corner     60px flat
 *   eyebrow    no font-size set here, so the wrapper's 11px → 10.5px step at
 *              520 is the whole curve; the .14em tracking this file sets is the
 *              design's and is the same value the wrapper drops to at 520, so
 *              nothing moves.
 * At 360px nothing may push sideways: every grid track is minmax(0,…), the
 * caption is absolutely positioned inside a clipped plate AND still takes
 * overflow-wrap: anywhere, and the eyebrow and headline take it too so one long
 * owner word — a hyphenless place name, a pasted URL — wraps instead of
 * widening the document. (The wrapper's `body { overflow-x: hidden }` would
 * CLIP such a word rather than scroll to it, so the tail of it would simply be
 * gone.) There is no button in this section, so R8's full-width
 * `white-space: normal` + `overflow-wrap: anywhere` pair has nothing to apply
 * to.
 *
 * ── CONTRAST, COMPUTED from PageBO's own token hexes (sRGB relative luminance,
 * WCAG 2.x) rather than copied from its table — the method was checked against
 * two of the wrapper's own figures first and reproduces BOTH exactly, with no
 * disagreement to explain away (ink/paper 15.92, accent/paper 5.63):
 *   eyebrow    --accent-deep on --lilac    7.40:1  (7.3956 unrounded)
 *   headline   --ink on --paper           15.92:1  (15.9234 unrounded)
 *   emphasis   --accent on --paper         5.63:1  (5.6263; the wrapper's .h2 em)
 *   caption    --ink on --paper           15.92:1  (an OPAQUE pill, so this is
 *                                                   the real pairing over any
 *                                                   photograph)
 * Nothing in this section is below AA, and no pair here puts the purple on the
 * pink — the one combination this palette cannot survive.
 */
// `layout` is accepted because PageBO passes it to every section uniformly;
// this block reads nothing outside content.*.
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

const gallery = (content && content.gallery) || {};
// An array at content.gallery would make these undefined, which is correct:
// the legacy bare-array form carries no tag and no headline.
const tag = typeof gallery.tag === 'string' ? gallery.tag.trim() : '';
const headline = typeof gallery.headline === 'string' ? gallery.headline.trim() : '';

// The legacy bare-array form lives at content.gallery ITSELF — not the
// `gallery` alias above, which is the object form's home. Both shapes reach
// production, so both are read.
const rawTiles: any[] = Array.isArray(gallery.items)
  ? gallery.items
  : (Array.isArray(content && content.gallery) ? content.gallery : []);

// Blank rows go; the ORIGINAL index travels with every survivor (R6).
const tiles = rawTiles
  .map((row: any, i: number) => {
    // An older draft has stored a bare path where the schema's newItem creates
    // { image, caption }. Read as an image rather than dropped.
    const item = typeof row === 'string'
      ? { image: row, caption: '' }
      : (row && typeof row === 'object' ? row : { image: '', caption: '' });
    return {
      image: String((item as any).image ?? '').trim(),
      caption: String((item as any).caption ?? '').trim(),
      i,
    };
  })
  .filter((t) => t.image.length > 0);

const n = tiles.length;

// CSS-only escape; the raw value still goes into data-image-field.
const cssUrl = (s: string) => s.replace(/'/g, '%27');

/* THE DESIGN'S CORNER RHYTHM, by DISPLAY position (R6): one corner of tiles 2,
   3, 5 and 8 is opened to 60px, the rest stay on the shared 26px. The run is
   eight long — the design's own slot count — so a ninth photograph restarts it
   instead of falling off the end. '' means "all four corners at --r-lg". */
const CORNERS = ['', 'br', 'tl', '', 'tr', '', '', 'bl'];

/* Row completion, per breakpoint (see THE COUNT LADDER above).
   FOUR tracks: 1–3 photos narrow the grid to their own count, so only 5+ can
   leave a partial row; the trailing tile then stretches over the tracks its row
   did not fill — remainder 1 spans all four, 2 spans three, 3 spans two.
   TWO tracks (≤900): an odd count leaves the trailing tile alone, and it
   stretches across the pair. */
const rem4 = n > 4 ? n % 4 : 0;
const wideDesk = rem4 === 1 ? 4 : (rem4 === 2 ? 3 : (rem4 === 3 ? 2 : 0));
const wideMid = (n > 2 && n % 2 === 1) ? 2 : 0;

// R5 — BODY content only. tag and headline are excluded: both are derived onto
// every site, so counting them would make this gate always-true.
const hasSection = n > 0;
---
{hasSection && (
  <section class="sip-gal" id="gallery">
    <div class="wrap">
      {(tag || headline) && (
        <div class="sip-gal-head reveal">
          {tag && <span class="kick sip-gal-kick" data-field="gallery.tag">{tag}</span>}
          {headline && <h2 class="h2 sip-gal-h" data-field="gallery.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
        </div>
      )}

      <div
        class:list={[
          'sip-gal-grid',
          'reveal',
          n === 1 && 'sip-gal-x1',
          n === 2 && 'sip-gal-x2',
          n === 3 && 'sip-gal-x3',
        ]}
        data-delay="1"
      >
        {tiles.map((t, pos) => (
          <figure
            class:list={[
              'plate',
              'sip-gal-frame',
              /* Decorative, so keyed off the DISPLAY position, never t.i. */
              pos % 2 === 1 && 'sip-gal-drop',
              CORNERS[pos % 8] && `sip-gal-${CORNERS[pos % 8]}`,
              pos === n - 1 && wideDesk === 4 && 'sip-gal-w4',
              pos === n - 1 && wideDesk === 3 && 'sip-gal-w3',
              pos === n - 1 && wideDesk === 2 && 'sip-gal-w2',
              pos === n - 1 && wideMid === 2 && 'sip-gal-wm2',
            ]}
          >
            <div
              class="ph"
              data-image-field={`gallery.items.${t.i}.image`}
              role={t.caption ? 'img' : undefined}
              aria-label={t.caption || undefined}
              aria-hidden={t.caption ? undefined : 'true'}
              style={`background-image:url('${cssUrl(t.image)}');`}
            ></div>
            {t.caption && (
              <figcaption class="cap sip-gal-cap" data-field={`gallery.items.${t.i}.caption`}>{t.caption}</figcaption>
            )}
          </figure>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* .sip-gal carries NO rule on purpose. PageBO's shared `section` rhythm
     already states this design's 80px top (capped there, and symmetric by
     contract) and .wrap already states its 1200px / 92vw. The class stays in
     the markup as this section's root hook. Read the frontmatter before adding
     a bare bottom here: a padding-bottom:0 would make this the one section that
     butts into the next, because every sibling in the variant leans on the
     wrapper's rhythm. */

  /* THE CENTRED HEAD. The design's 30px down to the grid sits on the BLOCK, not
     on the h2's own margin-bottom, so an eyebrow with no headline still clears
     the photographs instead of gluing them to its underside. */
  .sip-gal-head {
    text-align: center;
    margin: 0 0 30px;
  }

  /* THE EYEBROW. The wrapper's .kick already carries the design's type — 11px,
     700, uppercase, inline-block — and owns the whole size curve (it steps to
     10.5px at 520), so no font-size is written here. What .kick does NOT know
     is that in THIS design the eyebrow is a PILL: a lilac ground, the deep
     purple label, 8px/16px of padding and the 100px corner. Those four and the
     design's .14em tracking (the wrapper's base is .18em) are the only things
     restated. The label is --accent-deep and not --accent because that is what
     the design writes; it also happens to be the darker of the two, which this
     ground can afford. Qualified with .sip-gal-head so it out-specifies the
     wrapper's :where() primitive whatever order the sheets land in. */
  .sip-gal-head .sip-gal-kick {
    letter-spacing: .14em;
    color: var(--accent-deep);
    background-color: var(--lilac);
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border-radius: var(--r-pill);
    max-width: 100%;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* `.24em 0 0` verbatim; nothing below it (the block owns that). The wrapper's
     22ch cap is RELEASED because this head is centred: a 22ch block with
     `margin:… 0 0` would hug the left edge of the wrap with its text centred
     inside a narrow column. The design gives this h2 no max-width, so neither
     does this file. No font-size is set — the wrapper's shared clamp and its
     shared 760 pass own the whole size curve, and the purple emphasis is the
     wrapper's global `.h2 em` rule, which a scoped selector could not reach
     anyway because set:html builds that <em> after this sheet was scoped. */
  .sip-gal-head .sip-gal-h {
    margin: .24em 0 0;
    max-width: none;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── THE SCATTERED FOUR-UP ────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The design's tracks verbatim: four equal columns, 14px apart. The three
     knobs below are the design's own numbers, kept as custom properties so the
     breakpoints move the stagger without restating the grid, and so the one
     length in this template that has no token (60px) is named rather than
     sprinkled through four rules.
     minmax(0,…) on every track is the DESIGN's own value, kept verbatim, and it
     is also the safe one: a bare 1fr floors at min-content, so any future
     IN-FLOW child of a tile could widen its track past its share. Today's
     caption is absolutely positioned and so contributes nothing to min-content
     — this is belt, not brace, and it is here because the design put it here. */
  .sip-gal-grid {
    --sip-gal-h: 250px;
    --sip-gal-drop: 26px;
    --sip-gal-corner: 60px;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 14px;
  }
  /* THE COUNT LADDER, short end. Each tier restates the tracks for its own
     count so a three-photo gallery is three full tiles and not three tiles
     beside a hole. Two compounds plus Astro's scope, so each out-specifies the
     base grid above AND the collapse rules below — wherever a tier is already
     narrow enough to survive the collapse. */
  .sip-gal-grid.sip-gal-x1 { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .sip-gal-grid.sip-gal-x2 { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  .sip-gal-grid.sip-gal-x3 { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }

  /* THE TILE. Ground (--well), the shared 26px corner and the clip all arrive
     with .plate, which is what the design specifies per tile — so only geometry
     is set here. `margin: 0` is load-bearing: a <figure> carries a 1em/40px UA
     margin that would blow the grid apart. The wrapper's 180px .plate floor
     never binds at 250px, but it is released so the design's height is the one
     source of truth for it.
     ON THE .sip-gal-grid QUALIFIER — the frontmatter has the full working. This
     build scopes with `[data-astro-cid-…]` (Astro's 'attribute' default), so
     this rule ships at (0,4,0) against the wrapper's
